// ------------------------------------------------------------
// NEW: the rule itself
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// UPDATED (per product feedback, 2026-09-10): a rule now covers a LIST
// of match values, not one. Reason: a real category like "alcohol" or
// "tobacco" maps to several NCM codes in Brazil's official tax
// classification (beer, wine, spirits are different codes under
// chapter 22, for example) — the product owner does not want a
// company admin to have to look up and manually add every individual
// code as a separate rule. One rule = one CATEGORY, backed by a list
// of codes/keywords, any of which can trigger a match.
//
// OPEN QUESTION FOR THE TEAM: ASYN_Overview.docx says the database is
// MySQL. Prisma's native `String[]` list type is only supported on
// PostgreSQL/CockroachDB, NOT MySQL — so storing a list here needs one
// of two real approaches, and I have not been told which fits the
// existing project conventions better:
//
//   (a) A `Json` column (`matchValues Json`) — simplest, matches how
//       this demo's in-memory repository already treats it (a plain
//       array). Downside: the database can't efficiently query
//       "which rules contain code X" — you'd have to load rows and
//       filter in application code.
//
//   (b) A separate child table, e.g. `ProductRuleValue { id, ruleId,
//       value }` with a foreign key back to ProductRule. More
//       relational, indexable, and queryable — closer to how the rest
//       of this schema already models one-to-many relationships
//       (see InvoiceItem below). More migration/query code to write.
//
// This demo's in-memory repository stores matchValues as a plain JS
// array either way, so the choice below doesn't affect anything
// upstream of the repository layer — only `productRuleRepository.js`
// would change. Sketched as (a) below since it's the simpler default;
// flag this as a real decision to confirm, not a settled one.
enum ProductRuleMatchType {
  NCM_PREFIX
  KEYWORD
}

model ProductRule {
  id          String               @id @default(cuid())
  companyId   String
  name        String               // shown to the app user in the warning
  matchType   ProductRuleMatchType
  matchValues Json                 // array of strings, e.g. ["2203","2204","2208"] or ["cerveja","vinho"] — see OPEN QUESTION above
  description String?
  active      Boolean              @default(true)
  createdAt   DateTime             @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime             @updatedAt

  company     Company              @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  // A rule name/description is free text set by the company admin,
  // not a source of truth for anything besides that company's own
  // matching + the warning text — no other table should ever join
  // against this table to *display* historical results (see
  // InvoiceItem.blockedByRuleName below for why).

  @@index([companyId, active])
}

